The second version of the User Guide differed only in having a longer
(3 page) `addenda/errata' section at the start. This is an ASCII
version of it, provided for anyone who has one of the original 10,000
manuals (which have a 2-page addenda/errata, one page of which is for
the Programming manual).



Addenda and errata to the User Guide
====================================

***  World

p24: An `am' or `pm' indicator is ALWAYS shown next to the clock 
which shows the city's time.

p26: When you lock to a particular country, you can only use the
right arrow key to skip from one city to the next. Most other keys -
even Menu, for example - remove the country lock.

p27/p203: The best "quick" way to handle summer times is to think 
of World as two hemispheres - Northern/European combined, and Southern. 
When clocks go forward in your Home city, set your hemisphere `On', 
and the other `Off'. When clocks go back again, set your hemisphere 
`Off', and the other `On'.

***  The Calculator

p35: Powers are calculated from right to left, so 2**3**2 is 512 (2**9) 
not 64 (8**2).

p40: The `Exponential' format is now called `Scientific'.

***  The Database

p62: Note that the `Example programs' chapter in the Programming
Manual contains a program to insert a new blank line in every entry
in a database.

***  The Agenda

p79: You CAN set an alarm for an item which is before the current 
date and time, but the alarm will not ring.

p78/p202: Since you cannot set every day to be a rest day, you cannot 
`lose' Agenda items which have `workday' repeats.

***  The Word Processor

p93: Note that if you do want to start a new line without starting 
a new paragraph - when typing an address, for example - use Shift-Enter.

p105: The Word Processor can handle up to 40K of text in each file. 
(There are only a few rare exceptions to this - such as when you have 
copied or deleted a very large area of text.) If you try to open a 
file which is larger than this, a message such as `Overflow' will 
be displayed.

p118: The higher the outline level of a paragraph, the more it is 
shown indented.

p121: If you merge in a plain text file, as opposed to an ordinary 
Word Processor document, you will avoid any possible clashes of style 
and/or emphasis names.

p122: If you use `Save as template' and give `default' as the 
filename to use (on the default disk - usually `internal'), this template 
will be used whenever no other template is specified. For example, 
it will always be used when opening a document from the System screen, 
as there is no way to specify any other template.

***  Dialling

p132: The `Dial' keypress is Psion-Help, not Shift-Help.

***  Printing

p147: New documents which do not use a template are now given a default 
printer setup. They do NOT inherit the settings of the previous 
document.

***  Opening files

p155: You can also create and use a new file in the System screen 
without causing some other open file to close. Use the `New file' 
option, and type the name for the new file, but use Shift-Enter to 
complete the dialog.

p155: If you select the `Options' menu option in the System screen 
and set `Open multiple files' to `Enter', the System screen will NOT
close down an open file when you open another.

***  Files and disks

When setting a `Disk' line in a dialog, the choices given are the 
internal disk, the SSD drives `A' and `B', AND DRIVE `C'
for the expansion socket. This will usually be shown as `Absent'. 
Some expansion devices, such as Serial 3 Link, do contain a 
drive `C' which has files on it; Parallel 3 Link does not 
contain a drive `C'.

p165: If you select the `Options' menu option in the System screen 
and set `Update lists' to `System button', the System screen will 
update its file lists only when you press the System button. If you 
use Flash SSDs, this may also improve battery life slightly.

p169: The more you delete or modify database entries or agenda items 
in a Flash SSD file, the longer it will take to search through the 
file. You may wish occasionally to re-copy the database and agenda 
files on the SSD, using the `Save as' option, to improve the 
speed of searching.

p171: Word Processor templates are stored in \WDR - no \DEF directory 
is used.

p172: The file selector now shows the times of files, as well as their 
dates.

p172: If you use a Shift key with the letters you type, the file selector 
matches with directories. If you use the Control key instead, it matches 
with drives (so, for example, Control-B goes straight to drive `B').

p178: When copying an SSD, first close any open files which are on 
that disk.

The System screen has a `Directory' option, which lists files in the 
same way as Tab.

***  Other

If you use the `am-pm' time format, note that midnight is written 
with a 12, not a 00 - for example, half past midnight is 12:30 am.

In the Database, Agenda and World, the `Open' and `New' options are 
now called `Open file' and `New file'.

p16/p93: PSION-Delete deletes to the beginning of a line, and
Shift-PSION-Delete deletes to the end of a line. (You can also use
Shift-Control-PSION and the left arrow key to highlight the current
word, and Shift-Control-PSION and the right arrow key to highlight
the current paragraph.)

p198: `Auto turn off' is now part of an `Options' menu option in the 
System screen. The maximum value allowed is 17:59:59.

p204: The current date and time may be lost by a soft reset.

You can `kill' (close without saving data) any open application with 
the `Kill application' option in the System screen. This is a rather 
severe "low-level" operation, designed primarily to handle any rogue 
external application which locks up.

